          "question": "Does exercise help knee osteoarthritis or make it worse?",
          "answer": "The retrieved material partly answers this. It does not say directly whether exercise makes knee osteoarthritis worse.\n\nThe main source is an educational summary titled \"Exercising with osteoarthritis: what the program contains and how it feels.\" It says an exercise program for osteoarthritis combines three things: strengthening the muscles around the joint (for the knee, the quadriceps and hip muscles), range-of-movement work to keep the joint moving, and aerobic activity for general fitness and body weight. It also says that supervision at the start matters, because the right movements, load and progression are hard to judge alone.\n\nThe results did not cover whether exercise could worsen knee osteoarthritis, which exercises might cause problems, or how much pain during exercise is acceptable. A separate osteoarthritis guideline note is referenced in that summary, but it was not returned, so I can't report what it says.\n\nSince this is a question about your own knee, a physiotherapist, doctor or other clinician is the right person to ask. They can look at your specific situation and suggest a suitable plan.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
